A rainbow table attack is a type of cryptanalysis that involves using a precomputed table of results to speed up the cracking of hashed passwords. A rainbow table is created by taking a set of hashed passwords and their corresponding plaintext values, and then computing the hash of each computed hash value. The resulting table can then be used to quickly look up the plaintext value associated with a given hash value. However, creating a rainbow table from a large set of hashed passwords is computationally expensive and time consuming, so this type of attack is generally only used when other methods of cracking the passwords have failed. Additionally, modern hash functions are designed to make rainbow table attacks impractical by using complex algorithms that make the computation of new hash values too expensive for an attacker to perform in a reasonable amount of time. As such, rainbow table attacks are generally considered to be a relic of the past and are rarely used today. 
